<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Courland</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kurzeme</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Courland is a historical region of western Latvia, lying between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-riga">Gulf of Riga</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> and including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liepaja">Liepāja</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ventspils">Ventspils</a>. Conquered by the Livonian Order in the thirteenth century, it became the Duchy of Courland and Semigallia under Polish-Lithuanian suzerainty in 1561 and passed to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> in 1795. Predominantly Lutheran, it has formed part of Latvia since the country's independence.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/courland.jpg" alt="Courland" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Edgars Šulcs · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ventspils_viduslaiku_pils_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
